<Layout title="Research Philosophy">
  <p class="section-label">Philosophy</p>
  <h1>Research Philosophy</h1>
  <p>A lot of the things we care about can't be observed directly.</p>
  <p>
  Whether studying trauma, child development, or neural networks, we're usually limited to indirect measurements like behavior, test responses, neural activity, or model outputs. 
  The scientific challenge is to recover the hidden process that generated those observations. </p>
  <p>That question first appeared in my work on transgenerational trauma, where behavioral changes suggested underlying epigenetic mechanisms that could not be measured directly. 
  It resurfaced while developing a shortened developmental assessment, where latent cognitive abilities had to be inferred from hundreds of behavioral test items. 
  Now, it motivates my work in computational neuroscience, where I study how recurrent neural networks organize internal representations while solving adaptive decision-making tasks.</p>

  <h2>What kinds of questions excite me</h2>
  <p>I enjoy problems where the observable behavior is simple but the underlying computation is not. 
  I'm fascinated whenever multiple organisms or algorithms produce nearly identical outputs while relying on fundamentally different internal representations. 
  Understanding those hidden computations and learning how to recover them is the motivation that connects most of my work.</p>

  <h2>Current questions</h2>
  <p>How do recurrent neural networks organize their hidden-state geometry while solving sequential decision-making tasks?</p>
  <p>Which analyses genuinely reveal computation (fixed points, basin structure, manifold geometry)?</p>
  <p>Can latent-state models such as GLM-HMMs recover meaningful computational strategies, or are they mostly descriptive?</p>
  <p>How should we compare the internal computations of biological brains and artificial neural networks?</p>

  <h2>Open problems</h2>
  <p>I'm also interested in understanding which latent representations remain consistent across independently trained networks. 
  If multiple models solve the same task with similar behavior but different internal dynamics, what aspects of computation are truly fundamental?
  Longer term, I want to understand how these ideas scale from simplified behavioral tasks to richer neural population recordings and eventually to closed-loop neurotechnology.</p>

  <h2>Things I've changed my mind about</h2>
  <h3>1. Trusting beautiful visualizations too quickly</h3>
  <p>While analyzing hidden-state manifolds, I initially assumed that visually compelling PCA trajectories reflected meaningful computation. 
  Repeatedly checking my analyses taught me that attractive plots are easy to generate, and surprisingly easy to misinterpret. 
  I've become much more careful about validating every visualization against the underlying data and model assumptions.</p>
  <h3>2. Assuming that agreement implied identical computation</h3>
  <p>Some of the most productive parts of my projects have started with results that looked wrong. 
  An unexpected transition matrix, a strange latent trajectory, or a model behaving differently than intuition predicted has often forced me to reconsider my assumptions. 
  I've learned to treat surprising results as opportunities to understand the system more deeply rather than simply trying to make them disappear.</p>
</Layout>
